            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>When your ad account gets suspended with balance still sitting there but campaigns unable to run, it's one of the most dreaded scenarios for overseas advertising teams. Appeals aren't mystical, but going in with the wrong material preparation wastes time and may even result in escalated bans due to "repeated violations." Below are the 6 most frequently asked questions when we assist clients with appeals.</p>
<h2>Should I Check the Violation Reason or File an Appeal First?</h2>
<p><strong>Check the reason first, then write your appeal.</strong></p>
<p>Log into your Google Ads backend and check the "Policy Decisions" page to understand which specific ads and policies were violated. Common suspension reasons include: inaccessible landing pages, false advertising, circumventing systems, or non-compliant privacy policies. If you're unsure about the specific violation, search the Google Ads Policy Center for the relevant条款. Clarifying the violation is the foundation for preparing appeal materials; otherwise, no matter how sincere your explanation letter is, you may be addressing the wrong issue.</p>
<h2>What's the Most Critical Part of Appeal Materials?</h2>
<p><strong>The quality of your Statement of Facts.</strong></p>
<p>Many people spend extensive time preparing screenshots when appealing, but the reviewer actually first looks at whether your understanding of the violation is accurate and whether your corrective measures are specific. An effective statement needs to include: 1) acknowledgment or clarification of the specific violation; 2) explanation of what caused it (e.g., insufficient team training, oversight by a new team member); 3) corrective actions and preventive mechanisms already implemented. Vague statements like "We'll be more careful in the future" aren't convincing. You need specific actions.</p>
<h2>How to Write a Violation Explanation That Doesn't Look Like a Template?</h2>
<p><strong>Replace adjectives with specific numbers and actions.</strong></p>
<p>For example, instead of "We've strengthened ad creative review," write: "We've added a third-level review checkpoint in our launch process. All landing pages must pass a 12-item compliance checklist before going live, with review records retained for 90 days." Reviewers see numerous appeals daily. The key to distinguishing "boilerplate" from "genuine correction" is verifiable specifics. If you have policy training records or quality score improvements, attach those too.</p>
<h2>First Appeal Was Rejected. Can I Appeal Again? How Long Should I Wait?</h2>
<p><strong>Yes, but don't repeat appeals frequently.</strong></p>
<p>Google Ads typically allows resubmission, but each rejection is recorded. If your first appeal was rejected, carefully review the rejection reason. Determine if it was due to insufficient materials or fundamental violations (e.g., fraud or malicious behavior). The latter is usually very difficult to overturn. Wait at least 5-7 business days between appeals. During that time, complete your corrections and submit new materials. Filing the same reason three or more times may flag your account as "harassment appeals," negatively impacting future reviews.</p>
<h2>Account Suspended Due to "Linked Account" Issues. How to Appeal?</h2>
<p><strong>Distinguish between the main account and linked accounts, handle separately.</strong></p>
<p>Linked account suspensions typically occur in two scenarios: either your account shares login credentials, payment methods, or tracking codes with an account that has existing violations, or the website you're advertising on is linked to other suspended domains. When appealing, clearly state: the actual relationship between you and the suspended account (if any), and the isolation measures you've already taken, such as changing payment accounts, discontinuing shared tracking tools, or removing third-party scripts from your website. Providing before/after screenshots of isolation measures helps.</p>
<h2>What About the Balance and Campaign Data During the Appeal Period?</h2>
<p><strong>Balance can't be transferred temporarily, data should be backed up in advance.</strong></p>
<p>Google Ads balance cannot be withdrawn or transferred to other accounts during suspension. After a successful appeal, the account resumes and the balance remains usable. If the appeal period is lengthy (e.g., over two weeks), export important reports like campaign performance and audience data beforehand. The system won't automatically restore historical data after successful appeals—backing up is something only you can do.</p>
<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Account suspension isn't scary; repeatedly appealing with the wrong approach is. The core principles are simple: <strong>Understand the violation reason before writing your appeal, and replace empty promises with concrete actions.</strong> If your account involves linked suspensions or complex violations, organize all linked account information before submitting collectively to avoid rejection from scattered materials.</p>
